<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- GP-1 (.superpowers/sdd/2026-07-28-wsg-plugin-connector-seam-blueprint/task-1-brief.md) — the driver
         contract has to be a plain, dependency-free assembly a third-party vendor can compile a driver
         against outside this repo. St4i.EdgeCore is net10.0-windows (DPAPI CredentialStore, a
         WPF-adjacent vendored SDK) and is never published, so this project exists to be the thing that
         IS publishable later. Plain net10.0 — NOT -windows — is load-bearing: it is the whole reason
         this assembly exists. -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <!-- N-2 (.superpowers/sdd/abstractions-documented/task-1-brief.md), completing N-1's scope — the
         compiler parses this project's `///` blocks and resolves every symbol they name. Set per-project
         on purpose: Directory.Build.props carries the scope, the measured price for all fifteen projects,
         and why the remaining eight are an owner decision rather than an implementer one.

         WHY THIS ROW WAS THE ONE TO MOVE, and it is N-1's own argument, not a new one: this is the only
         documented surface in this tree with readers OUTSIDE this organisation — the contract a
         third-party driver author compiles against and reads first — it declares no NuGet package and no
         reference to any other project in this repository, deliberately, so they can. N-1 selected its six
         by what the coverage assertion COST, named that as the honest weakness of its own scope, and left
         this row unguarded. The bill here — 95 doc comments, measured and re-measured before it was
         paid — was written, not suppressed; its parse/resolve column was already zero because N-1 fixed
         all 16 sites here while the switch was still off, so this stayed a one-property change.

         🔴 AND THAT SENTENCE IS PHRASED THE WAY IT IS BECAUSE A TEST CAUGHT THE FIRST DRAFT, which is
         worth leaving here rather than in a report. Its first version quoted the two element names
         verbatim from the paragraph in Directory.Build.props that makes this argument. That turned the
         gate RED: in ZeroDependencyTests there is a test that reads THIS FILE with File.ReadAllText and
         then calls Assert.DoesNotContain for each of those two names over the raw text — so it cannot tell
         a declaration from a mention inside a comment, and a task whose entire product is prose is exactly
         where that difference shows up. The assertion is not wrong about anything it exists to protect
         (this project still declares neither); it is simply measuring text, and it fails in the safe
         direction.

         🔴 That test is identified here by its CLASS and by WHAT IT DOES, and deliberately not by its
         method name, which is a partial exception to §8.1(h8)'s "point by NAME, never by POSITION" — and
         the exception is the rule's own subject matter, not a lapse. Its method name CONTAINS one of the
         two forbidden substrings, so writing it here re-breaks the very assertion this paragraph is about;
         that was caught the same way as the first draft, by the gate, on the run after the fix. Pointing
         at the raw-text Assert.DoesNotContain rather than at an ordinal is the narrowest form that still
         survives someone inserting another test above it. Do not restate any of this using those two
         names. -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
